CeeSafe runtime exception

Copyright (C) 2026 Veeran Puthumkara.

CeeSafe is licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 2 only (COPYING). It forked from Nova, which is
licensed the same way, with the same exception. Some of its code ends up inside every program that CeeSafe compiles.
So that compiling a program with CeeSafe does not by itself place that program under the GPL, that code carries the
exception below, as the C library of a GPL compiler would.

The exception applies to:

  - the C runtime: ceesafe/rt.h and the ceesafe_rt_*.c files, which ceesafec embeds (src/library/runtime.cee) and
    writes next to every program's generated C;
  - the CeeSafe core library, lib/core (embedded in src/library/sources.cee, its sources in tests/reference/lib/core/),
    compiled into every package;
  - the C code ceesafec writes as its output (generated main.c, ceesafe_exports.h and the like), to the extent that it
    contains code from ceesafec itself rather than from the program being compiled.

In those files, "this library" in the exception means the files listed above. Their SPDX identifier is
GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0. Everything else in this repository (the compiler, its tools and tests, the
editor support, the specification and the documentation) is GPL-2.0-only without the exception.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Linking this library statically or dynamically with other modules is making a combined work based on this
library. Thus, the terms and conditions of the GNU General Public License cover the whole combination.

As a special exception, the copyright holders of this library give you permission to link this library with
independent modules to produce an executable, regardless of the license terms of these independent modules,
and to copy and distribute the resulting executable under terms of your choice, provided that you also meet,
for each linked independent module, the terms and conditions of the license of that module. An independent
module is a module which is not derived from or based on this library. If you modify this library, you may
extend this exception to your version of the library, but you are not obligated to do so. If you do not wish
to do so, delete this exception statement from your version.
